{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>See.</b> He is still speaking about the matter of the woman, and the word \"said Qohelet\" is in the language of wisdom, and the meaning of חשבון is thought, and the subject is that a man cannot find a thought that he desires until he joins one to another, such that it is known that the first combination is subject and predicate, and likewise the second combination; only when they are joined, predicate first and subject last, or two predicates, or two subjects equally, then a third notion is generated between the two combinations. And so it is in matters of the soul, and even in corporeal entities. And the subject is that it is not fitting for a wise man to associate with a woman at all, were it not that it is impossible to leave his likeness after him — which is a third — except through the association of father and mother. And another interpretation: this verse is joined with what follows it, and the meaning of the word חשבון is thought or counting, and the subject is: when you consider every thing, you find it is not alone, but must be joined to another, like great and small — for it is not called great except in contrast to something smaller than it, and the small one likewise. And so wise and fool, and so righteous and wicked — also in calculation. For when one is joined to another, the head of the count arises, and if one is joined to head, it returns like end, and when one is joined to end, it returns as closed, and when one is joined to closed, it returns as round, and the round is straightened, and the straightened is whole, and the whole is a body. And the subject: even if no man of understanding and wisdom and fear is found — he is the straight man — rather he is found only in contrast to other human beings, and among males alone one is found out of a thousand. And the word אחת is feminine. And know that the letters are divided into two groups: half serve sometimes and sometimes they are principal, and the serving ones are שתי\"ל א\"ב המו\"ן, and the rest are always principal, and they are not found added except by reduplication, like שחרחורת, and likewise they are not subtracted except from the forms of reduplication, as when the דל\"ת of שדד is subtracted from שוד. And likewise when the root תי\"ו joins with the תי\"ו of [2fs] presence, the root [תי\"ו] is missing, like אותו תשחית וכרת. And likewise when the נון of the root joins with the נו\"ן of many females, the root נו\"ן is missing, like בצל דליותיו תשכונה, and it is swallowed. And likewise the word משרת את המלך: on account of the joining of the root תי\"ו with the feminine תי\"ו, they removed the root תי\"ו and vocalized the רי\"ש with pataḥ, to indicate feminine language, to distinguish it from משרת משה, which is masculine. And likewise when one דל\"ת — and it is weak — joins with a weak תי\"ו that is feminine, and they are close in articulation, they removed the דל\"ת which is a root, and this is the case of הרה ללת. And some say that two roots are in one matter, and they interpret אח לא פדה יפדה איש as: make אח from one of these, like אחד. And this is far-fetched, for the meaning of אח לא פדה is: a brother does not redeem a man's soul, or: no man redeems his brother's soul, and אח would be an agent or object, and what follows it indicates this, namely ויקר פדיון נפשם, and the subject is that human beings cannot redeem their brothers or their loved ones from death. And how could they redeem them, when they are unable to redeem their own souls? And the meaning of ועשה אח is agent or object, and the sense of agent is: one who does [it]; and the sense of object is: one who does a transgression. It is a sister to those transgressions mentioned, or an evil thing is a brother to them. And furthermore, how could the meaning of ועשה אח be \"one\"? And behold it would revert, because what follows it — מאחת מאלה — produces ועשה אחד מאחת."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The text is a dense run of grammatical and exegetical arguments with minimal punctuation;and follows the original's loose chain of ו/וכן connectors. 'תי\"ו הנכח' (the תי\"ו of presence) resolved as the second-person feminine suffix תֵי\"ו, following Ibn Ezra's standard terminology for suffixal ת. 'המו\"ן' read as the letters ה מ ו ן; 'שתי\"ל א\"ב' as ש ת י ל א ב — both lists of quasi-servile letters. 'הרי\"ש' is the letter ר. 'המיושר' translated 'straightened' to preserve the geometric progression from נגדר (closed) to עגול (round) to מיושר to שלם."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:28:35.054729077Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}